      "latency_ms": 2157.5,
      "prompt_tokens": 85,
      "completion_tokens": 769,
      "output": "**Water** is a simple chemical compound that is essential for life on Earth. Its basic characteristics are:\n\n| Aspect | Details |\n|--------|---------|\n| **Chemical formula** | **H₂O** – two hydrogen atoms covalently bonded to one oxygen atom. |\n| **Molecular structure** | Bent (angular) shape with a bond angle of about 104.5°, giving the molecule a permanent dipole (partial negative charge on oxygen, partial positive on hydrogens). |\n| **Physical states** | Exists naturally as **solid (ice), liquid (water), and gas (water vapor/steam)**. The transitions are: melting (0 °C), boiling (100 °C at 1 atm), sublimation, and deposition. |\n| **Key properties** | • **High specific heat** – can absorb a lot of heat without large temperature change, moderating climate.<br>• **High heat of vaporization** – requires much energy to evaporate, driving cooling processes (e.g., sweating).<br>• **Universal solvent** – polar nature dissolves many ionic and polar substances, making it the “solvent of life.”<br>• **Surface tension** – strong hydrogen‑bond network gives water a “skin” that allows insects to walk on it and plants to transport water upward.<br>• **Density anomaly** – ice is less dense than liquid water, so it floats, insulating bodies of water in winter. |\n| **Occurrence** | Covers ~71 % of Earth’s surface (≈ 97 % of which is saltwater in oceans; the rest is freshwater in lakes, rivers, glaciers, groundwater, and the atmosphere). |\n| **Biological role** | • **Medium for biochemical reactions** – enzymes, metabolism, and transport of nutrients occur in aqueous environments.<br>• **Structural component** – makes up ~60 % of the human body by weight; present in cells, blood, and extracellular fluids.<br>• **Regulates temperature** – through sweating, respiration, and water circulation. |\n| **Industrial & everyday uses** | Drinking, agriculture, sanitation, power generation (hydropower, steam turbines), chemical manufacturing, cooling systems, cleaning, food preparation, etc. |\n| **Environmental importance** | • **Hydrologic cycle** – evaporation, condensation, precipitation, runoff, and infiltration continuously recycle water.<br>• **Climate regulation** – oceans store and transport heat; water vapor is a potent greenhouse gas.<br>• **Habitat** – supports aquatic ecosystems and influences terrestrial ecosystems. |\n\n### Why water is unique\n1. **Hydrogen bonding**: Each water molecule can form up to four hydrogen bonds with neighbors, creating a dynamic, cohesive network that underlies many of its unusual properties (high surface tension, high heat capacity, etc.).\n2. **Polarity**: The uneven charge distribution allows water to interact with both charged ions (e.g., Na⁺, Cl⁻) and polar molecules (e.g., sugars, alcohols), making it an excellent solvent.\n3. **Phase flexibility**: The relatively narrow temperature range between solid, liquid, and gas phases at Earth‑like pressures enables the water cycle to operate efficiently.\n\n### Quick summary\n- **What it is**: A tiny, polar molecule (H₂O) held together by covalent bonds and extensive hydrogen bonding.\n- **Why it matters**: Its physical and chemical properties make it indispensable for life, climate, and countless human activities."
